    col
      text Download the source code and working demo for this guide from the . You can also  for live editing. primary
      text Want to skip to the end? primary
      link "twbs/examples repository" https://github.com/twbs/examples/tree/main/vite
      link "open the example in StackBlitz" https://stackblitz.com/github/twbs/examples/tree/main/vite?file=index.html
      h2 What is Vite?
      text is a modern frontend build tool designed for speed and simplicity. It provides an efficient and streamlined development experience, especially for modern JavaScript frameworks.
      link Vite https://vite.dev/
      h2 Setup
      text We’re building a Vite project with Bootstrap from scratch, so there are some prerequisites and upfront steps before we can really get started. This guide requires you to have Node.js installed and some familiarity with the terminal.
      text Create a project folder and set up npm. We'll create the my-project folder and initialize npm with the -y argument to avoid it asking us all the interactive questions.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text mkdir my-project && cd my-project npm init -y
      text Install Vite. Unlike our Webpack guide, there’s only a single build tool dependency here. We use --save-dev to signal that this dependency is only for development use and not for production.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text npm i --save-dev vite
      text Install Bootstrap. Now we can install Bootstrap. We'll also install Popper since our dropdowns, popovers, and tooltips depend on it for their positioning. If you don’t plan on using those components, you can omit Popper here.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text npm i --save bootstrap @popperjs/core
      text Install additional dependency. In addition to Vite and Bootstrap, we need another dependency (Sass) to properly import and bundle Bootstrap’s CSS.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text npm i --save-dev sass
      text Now that we have all the necessary dependencies installed and set up, we can get to work creating the project files and importing Bootstrap.
      h2 Project structure
      text We’ve already created the my-project folder and initialized npm. Now we'll also create our src folder, stylesheet, and JavaScript file to round out the project structure. Run the following from my-project, or manually create the folder and file structure shown below.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text mkdir {src,src/js,src/scss} touch src/index.html src/js/main.js src/scss/styles.scss vite.config.js
      text When you’re done, your complete project should look like this:
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text my-project/ ├── src/ │ ├── js/ │ │ └── main.js │ └── scss/ │ | └── styles.scss | └── index.html ├── package-lock.json ├── package.json └── vite.config.js
      text At this point, everything is in the right place, but Vite won’t work because we haven’t filled in our vite.config.js yet.
      h2 Configure Vite
      text With dependencies installed and our project folder ready for us to start coding, we can now configure Vite and run our project locally.
      text Open vite.config.js in your editor. Since it’s blank, we'll need to add some boilerplate config to it so we can start our server. This part of the config tells Vite where to look for our project’s JavaScript and how the development server should behave (pulling from the src folder with hot reload).
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text import { resolve } from 'path' export default { root: resolve(__dirname, 'src'), build: { outDir: '../dist' }, server: { port: 8080 }, // Optional: Silence Sass deprecation warnings. See note below. css: { preprocessorOptions: { scss: { silenceDeprecations: [ 'import', 'mixed-decls', 'color-functions', 'global-builtin', ], }, }, }, }
      text Sass deprecation warnings are shown when compiling source Sass files with the latest versions of Dart Sass. This does not prevent compilation or usage of Bootstrap. We’re , but in the meantime these deprecation notices can be ignored.
      text Note:
      link "working on a long-term fix" https://github.com/twbs/bootstrap/issues/40962
      text Next we fill in src/index.html. This is the HTML page Vite will load in the browser to utilize the bundled CSS and JS we'll add to it in later steps.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text "<!doctype html> <html lang=\"en\"> <head> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"> <title>Bootstrap w/ Vite</title> <script type=\"module\" src=\"./js/main.js\"></script> </head> <body> <div class=\"container py-4 px-3 mx-auto\"> <h1>Hello, Bootstrap and Vite!</h1> <button class=\"btn btn-primary\">Primary button</button> </div> </body> </html>"
      text "We’re including a little bit of Bootstrap styling here with the div class=\"container\" and <button> so that we see when Bootstrap’s CSS is loaded by Vite."
      text Now we need an npm script to run Vite. Open package.json and add the start script shown below (you should already have the test script). We'll use this script to start our local Vite dev server.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text "{ // ... \"scripts\": { \"start\": \"vite\", \"test\": \"echo \\\"Error: no test specified\\\" && exit 1\" }, // ... }"
      text And finally, we can start Vite. From the my-project folder in your terminal, run that newly added npm script:
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text npm start
      image https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/assets/img/guides/vite-dev-server.png "Vite dev server running"
      text In the next and final section to this guide, we’ll import all of Bootstrap’s CSS and JavaScript.
      h2 Import Bootstrap
      text Import Bootstrap’s CSS. Add the following to src/scss/styles.scss to import all of Bootstrap’s source Sass.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text "// Import all of Bootstrap’s CSS @import \"bootstrap/scss/bootstrap\";"
      text You can also import our stylesheets individually if you want.  for details.
      link "Read our Sass import docs" /docs/5.3/customize/sass#importing
      text Next we load the CSS and import Bootstrap’s JavaScript. Add the following to src/js/main.js to load the CSS and import all of Bootstrap’s JS. Popper will be imported automatically through Bootstrap.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text // Import our custom CSS import '../scss/styles.scss' // Import all of Bootstrap’s JS import * as bootstrap from 'bootstrap'
      text You can also import JavaScript plugins individually as needed to keep bundle sizes down:
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text import Alert from 'bootstrap/js/dist/alert'; // or, specify which plugins you need: import { Tooltip, Toast, Popover } from 'bootstrap';
      text for more information on how to use Bootstrap’s plugins.
      link "Read our JavaScript docs" /docs/5.3/getting-started/javascript/
      text And you’re done! 🎉 With Bootstrap’s source Sass and JS fully loaded, your local development server should now look like this:
      image https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/assets/img/guides/vite-dev-server-bootstrap.png "Vite dev server running with Bootstrap"
      text Now you can start adding any Bootstrap components you want to use. Be sure to  for how to include additional custom Sass and optimize your build by importing only the parts of Bootstrap’s CSS and JS that you need.
      link "check out the complete Vite example project" https://github.com/twbs/examples/tree/main/vite
      text See something wrong or out of date here? Please . Need help troubleshooting?  on GitHub.
      link "open an issue on GitHub" https://github.com/twbs/bootstrap/issues/new/choose
      link "Search or start a discussion" https://github.com/twbs/bootstrap/discussions
